---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Num from "../components/Num.astro";
import Leaderboard from "../components/Leaderboard.astro";
import { nativeGap, POPULARITY, byName, href } from "../lib/data";

const ease = byName("ease")!;
const masked = byName("masked-set")!;
---

<Base
  wide
  title="The vector is the lossy part"
  chapter="Three — the lossy part"
  standfirst="Two models here score four to five times better under their own rule than through the embeddings they export. The embedding is what almost everyone ships."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A recommender usually has two faces. Inside, it has some way of scoring
      one item against a set of others — a solved linear system, an attention
      pass, a conditional distribution. Outside, it has a table of vectors,
      one per item, which is what gets exported, cached, put in a vector
      database and shipped to whatever consumes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those two faces are not equally good, and the gap is not small.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="gap">
    <table>
      <caption class="label">
        Recall@10 · the same model, scored two ways
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Model</th>
          <th scope="col" class="r">As exported vectors</th>
          <th scope="col" class="r">Under its own rule</th>
          <th scope="col" class="r">Factor</th>
          <th scope="col"></th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {nativeGap.map((g) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>{g.model}</code></th>
            <td class="r"><Num value={g.embedding} tone="below" /></td>
            <td class="r"><Num value={g.native} tone="above" /></td>
            <td class="r"><Num value={g.ratio} dp={1} tone="accent" />×</td>
            <td class="viz">
              {/* Two ticks on a shared axis, with the baseline drawn between
                  them. Bars would imply the quantities add; ticks say only
                  "here, and also here", which is the actual claim. */}
              <svg viewBox="0 0 200 22" role="img"
                   aria-label={`embedding ${g.embedding.toFixed(4)}, native ${g.native.toFixed(4)}, baseline ${POPULARITY.toFixed(4)}`}>
                <line x1="0" y1="11" x2="200" y2="11" class="axis" />
                <line x1={POPULARITY * 280} y1="2" x2={POPULARITY * 280} y2="20"
                      class="base" />
                <circle cx={g.embedding * 280} cy="11" r="4" class="emb" />
                <circle cx={g.native * 280} cy="11" r="4" class="nat" />
                <line x1={g.embedding * 280} y1="11" x2={g.native * 280} y2="11"
                      class="link" />
              </svg>
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <figcaption class="label">
      Vertical rule is the popularity baseline at {POPULARITY.toFixed(4)}.
      Both models cross it only under their own rule.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <code>ease</code> is the clearest case. As an embedding it scores{" "}
      <Num value={ease.M6_recall_at_10} tone="below" /> — far below the
      baseline, and on the strength of that number you would discard it.
      Scored the way it actually works, by summing rows of its item-item
      matrix, it reaches{" "}
      <Num value={ease.M6_native_recall_at_10} tone="above" />. Same fitted
      model. Same held-out recipes.{" "}
      <b>The vectors lose {(ease.M6_native_recall_at_10! / ease.M6_recall_at_10! ).toFixed(1)}× of the model's ability.</b>
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>masked-set</code> behaves the same way and is the best model on
      the site at <Num value={masked.M6_native_recall_at_10} tone="above" />,
      while its exported vectors sit at{" "}
      <Num value={masked.M6_recall_at_10} tone="below" />, beneath the
      baseline.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this matters more than the leaderboard</h2>
    <p>
      The embedding column is the one that makes families comparable — it is
      the only thing a matrix factorisation, a graph network and a
      transformer all produce. So it is the column an evaluation naturally
      reports, and reporting it is how these two models end up looking like
      failures.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is also the column that describes production, because the vector
      table is what gets deployed. Both readings are defensible and they
      point in opposite directions, which is why this site reports both and
      sorts by the one the model would actually serve.
    </p>
    <p class="aside">
      The demo on the front page runs <code>ease</code> under its own rule,
      in your browser, on the item-item matrix itself — not on its
      embeddings. That is why it performs well there and badly in the
      embedding column here.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The full table, both columns</h2>
  <Leaderboard showEmbedding compact />

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="next"><a href={href("/metrics/")}>Continue: the metrics disagree →</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .gap { margin-block: var(--s-6) var(--s-7); overflow-x: auto; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse;
          font-family: var(--sans); font-size: var(--t-sm); }
  caption { caption-side: top; text-align: left; padding-bottom: var(--s-3);
            color: var(--ink-faint); }
  figcaption { margin-top: var(--s-3); color: var(--ink-faint);
               max-width: var(--measure); }
  thead th { text-align: left; font-size: var(--t-xs); font-weight: 600;
             letter-spacing: 0.07em; text-transform: uppercase;
             color: var(--ink-faint); padding: 0 var(--s-3) var(--s-2) 0;
             border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--ink-soft); }
  tbody th, tbody td { padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-3) var(--s-3) 0;
                       border-bottom: 1px solid var(--paper-edge); }
  tbody th { text-align: left; font-weight: 500; }
  .r { text-align: right; }
  .viz { width: 40%; min-width: 12rem; padding-right: 0 !important; }
  svg { width: 100%; height: 22px; display: block; overflow: visible; }
  .axis { stroke: var(--paper-edge); stroke-width: 1; }
  .base { stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .link { stroke: var(--ink-faint); stroke-width: 1; stroke-dasharray: 2 2; }
  .emb  { fill: var(--below); }
  .nat  { fill: var(--above); }

  code { background: var(--paper-sunk); padding: 0 0.25em; }
  .aside { border-left: 2px solid var(--rule); padding-left: var(--s-4);
           color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--t-base); }
  .next { margin-top: var(--s-6); font-size: var(--t-md); }
</style>
